\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Marshwiggle: Ezekiel chapter 4 starts with an 'and' which is almost adversative, contrasting Ezekiel with the rebellion of the house of Israel with which chapter 3 ended, and setting him immediately to the task of responding as God's prophet to that rebellion? Ezekiel's own rebellion in chapter 3 had been dealt with decisively, by repeated command, constraint, force, and above all the Spirit. But now, Ezekiel, addressed as he was in his commissioning as a son of man, a son of Adam, is to take a brick, and engrave on it the city of Jerusalem. Against this symbol for Jerusalem, Ezekiel is to lay a siege, with all the instruments of siege warfare at the time. So far, this symbol is a prediction that the city would be put under siege. But this symbol is the context for two shocking further symbolic messages. Ezekiel - and who does he stand for here, a symbol himself? - is to put an iron barrier between himself and the city. And he is to set his face against the city. What do you make of the contrast with which the chapter starts? Who is Ezekiel a symbol for, and what does that mean for these symbolic acts?\
Opus: The contrast marked by 
\f1 \uc0\u1493 \u1456 \u1488 \u1463 \u1514 \u1464 \u1468 \u1492 
\f0  ("and you") at 4:1 functions less as pure adversative and more as emphatic distinction. Chapter 3 closes with Ezekiel constrained to silence except when God opens his mouth - he cannot function as a 
\f1 \uc0\u1502 \u1493 \u1465 \u1499 \u1460 \u1497 \u1495 \u1463 
\f0  (reprover) to this rebellious house. The opening of chapter 4 doesn't reverse this constraint but channels it: since direct verbal reproof is blocked, Ezekiel must now embody the message through symbolic actions.\
The question of who Ezekiel represents in these acts resists simple answers. The siege implements and the act of besieging initially suggest he represents Babylon/Nebuchadnezzar. But the iron barrier (
\f1 \uc0\u1511 \u1460 \u1497 \u1512 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1489 \u1463 \u1468 \u1512 \u1456 \u1494 \u1462 \u1500 
\f0 ) between prophet and city complicates this reading. Why would a besieger need protection from the besieged?\
The phrase 
\f1 \uc0\u1493 \u1463 \u1492 \u1458 \u1499 \u1460 \u1497 \u1504 \u1465 \u1514 \u1464 \u1492 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1488 \u1462 \u1514 \u1470 \u1508 \u1464 \u1468 \u1504 \u1462 \u1497 \u1498 \u1464 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1488 \u1461 \u1500 \u1462 \u1497 \u1492 \u1464 
\f0  ("and set your face toward it") uses language often associated with divine judgment in prophetic literature. Combined with the iron barrier, this suggests Ezekiel may represent God himself - the barrier reflecting the theological reality that sin creates separation between God and his people (Isaiah 59:2 uses similar imagery).\
Yet verse 3 ends with "this is a sign for the house of Israel" - 
\f1 \uc0\u1488 \u1493 \u1465 \u1514 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1492 \u1460 \u1497 \u1488 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1500 \u1456 \u1489 \u1461 \u1497 \u1514 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1497 \u1460 \u1513 \u1456 \u1474 \u1512 \u1464 \u1488 \u1461 \u1500 
\f0 . The demonstrative "this" (
\f1 \uc0\u1492 \u1460 \u1497 \u1488 
\f0 ) is feminine, matching either the city or potentially the entire enacted scene. If Ezekiel represents God, then the sign would be: God himself has become Jerusalem's besieger, with an impenetrable barrier between them.\
The subsequent verses (4-8) where Ezekiel bears the iniquity of Israel and Judah by lying on his sides suggests yet another dimension - Ezekiel as representative of the people themselves, bearing their punishment. This creates a complex symbolic matrix where the prophet simultaneously enacts multiple roles in the theological drama of judgment.\
Marshwiggle: I am not quite following your logic - Ezekiel may not speak except as God opens his mouth, but God could order exactly that. Instead, God orders symbolic acts. To understand why, we will need to look at the message of those symbols. Similarly, I do not entirely agree with respect to the barrier. Much siege warfare technique is designed precisely around protecting besieger from besieged. But I agree that the symbolic siege might, before the barrier, give the impression that Ezekiel represents Babylon, the obvious besieger. I do find support for your interpretation that the barrier implies that Ezekiel represents God himself. First, he has just been introduced as standing in God's counsel and speaking for God. Second, as you say, set your face toward it is commonly used in that context. Third, there is the logic coming out of chapter 3, as the house of Israel is in rebellion against the LORD, their king, and the covenant which lays out the terms of that kingship specifically promises that the LORD will bring such an invasion if they rebel. The protections of that covenant only apply when they do not rebel. The barrier implies that even the possibility of petitioning for aid is foreclosed - not surprising, given the way the covenant lays out that possibility only following repentance which Ezekiel has already been told is not forthcoming. And so I think you are correct in your interpretation of this sign. But I think we should be more precise in our language concerning verse 4 through 8. Ezekiel bears iniquity - and that word can imply punishment, if only because of the way iniquity leads to punishment. But we would need evidence that punishment is in view here. Perhaps that is the case. Perhaps it is not. What evidence do you think there is for each possibility?\
Opus: You're right to demand precision about 
\f1 \uc0\u1506 \u1458 \u1493 \u1465 \u1503 
\f0  (iniquity) in verses 4-8. The verb 
\f1 \uc0\u1504 \u1464 \u1513 \u1464 \u1474 \u1488 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1506 \u1458 \u1493 \u1465 \u1503 
\f0  can mean bearing guilt, bearing sin itself, or bearing punishment - and the text doesn't explicitly clarify which is in view.\
Evidence suggesting punishment:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sl280\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The physical constraint and suffering involved (lying immobilized for 390+40 days)\
\ls1\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The parallel with siege conditions that follow (rationed food, unclean bread)\
\ls1\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The temporal correspondence (
\f1 \uc0\u1497 \u1493 \u1465 \u1501 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1500 \u1463 \u1513 \u1464 \u1468 \u1473 \u1504 \u1464 \u1492 
\f0  - "a day for a year") implies he experiences something proportional to their years of sin/judgment\
\ls1\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The cords (
\f1 \uc0\u1506 \u1458 \u1489 \u1493 \u1465 \u1514 \u1460 \u1497 \u1501 
\f0 ) in verse 8 that prevent him from turning suggest enforced suffering\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl280\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Evidence against punishment/for merely bearing iniquity:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sl280\partightenfactor0
\ls2\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 No explicit statement that this is punishment (contrast with texts that clearly state punishment)\
\ls2\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Ezekiel hasn't committed these sins - he's performing a symbolic act\
\ls2\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The focus may be on making visible the accumulated weight of sin rather than experiencing its penalty\
