\cf2 The strongest evidence seems to point toward Ezekiel making visible the weight of accumulated iniquity rather than undergoing vicarious punishment. The physical discomfort serves the symbol (you cannot turn away from 390 years of sin) rather than constituting penal suffering. The siege parallel supports this - he's demonstrating theological reality through physical representation.\
Marshwiggle: The 390 years goes back to Solomon, who introduced idol worship into Jerusalem and the Davidic monarchy. This at least gives a hint that the 390 days is a reference to wrongdoing, deserving of punishment, but not punishment as such. 40 years for forty days, however, has several possible references. The rain which flooded the earth in Noah's day. One of the periods Moses went up to meet with God on Sinai. The time the spies sent into Caanan spent. This last one has the advantage of a previous specific correspondence with 40 years. The people were kept out of the land for 40 years, a year for every day, as punishment for their rebellion - a whole generation of punishment until the rebels died. There is at least some connection between this kind of punishment and exile. Moreover, it is a reminder of the relevant truth that rebellion removes the right of the people to the land, as it did during those 40 years. How much weight do you think this evidence should be given, and how would you integrate that evidence?\
Opus: The Numbers 14:34 parallel carries substantial weight - the verbal and conceptual parallels are too precise to ignore. There we have 
\f1 \uc0\u1497 \u1493 \u1465 \u1501 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1500 \u1463 \u1513 \u1464 \u1468 \u1473 \u1504 \u1464 \u1492 
\f0  ("day for a year"), the verb 
\f1 \uc0\u1504 \u1464 \u1513 \u1464 \u1474 \u1488 
\f0  with 
\f1 \uc0\u1506 \u1458 \u1493 \u1465 \u1503 
\f0 , and critically, a judgment that prevented entry into the land. The connection to exile is unmistakable.\
This evidence shifts my reading. Ezekiel isn't experiencing vicarious punishment but rather demonstrating the theological arithmetic of covenant violation. The 390 days/years from Solomon's apostasy shows accumulated northern kingdom iniquity; the 40 days/years echoes the wilderness generation's forfeiture of land rights through rebellion.\
The integration point seems to be this: just as the spies' bad report led to 40 years of wilderness wandering before the people could enter the land, Judah's rebellion leads to a parallel period where they cannot remain in the land. Ezekiel's immobilized body makes visible what has been true spiritually - the weight of accumulated rebellion has reached its mathematical completion.\
This reading explains why Ezekiel must lie on his sides rather than simply announce numbers. His constrained body demonstrates that these aren't negotiable figures but fixed realities as certain as his inability to turn. The sins have been committed; the years have been counted; the consequence follows with mathematical certainty.\
The siege symbolism (verses 1-3) and the iniquity-bearing (verses 4-8) thus work together: God has become Jerusalem's besieger precisely because the accumulated iniquity has reached its full measure, triggering covenant curses as automatically as falling triggers impact.\
Marshwiggle: Moreover, the Ezekiel is told of this binding in similar language to how he was told of being bound in chapter 3, where the binding prevented him from going to the people. But in chapter 3, he was also told he would be unable to act as a mediator for the people. Ezekiel may, as a priest, represent the people in the matter of their iniquity. But he may not, as Moses did, act as a mediator for the people - though even Moses was not permitted to turn God aside from punishing rebellion in several instances, including the 40 years we just discussed. Though in Ezekiel, the nation has a man standing in the counsels of God, the punishment is inevitable. Thus, in its own way, Ezekiel's signs, in brick and in his own body, act as an explanation before the fact for the disasters that might otherwise be misunderstood, and as warnings before the fact that all the reasons someone might think this cannot happen will not be enough.\
Opus: The parallel between the binding language in chapters 3 and 4 creates a theological through-line: Ezekiel cannot be a 
\f1 \uc0\u1502 \u1493 \u1465 \u1499 \u1460 \u1497 \u1495 \u1463 
\f0  (reprover) wandering among the people (3:25-26), cannot turn from side to side while bearing iniquity (4:8), and crucially, cannot function as mediator despite his priestly status. The repetition of constraint emphasizes foreclosed possibilities.\
The Moses comparison sharpens this. Even Moses - who turned aside divine wrath after the golden calf incident - could not prevent the 40-year judgment after the spies' report. He could plead that the people not be destroyed entirely (Numbers 14:13-19), but the generational punishment stood. Ezekiel's situation is more severe: he's not even positioned to attempt intercession. The iron wall between him and the city (4:3) symbolically demonstrates this impossibility.\
