These signs prevent a crucial misunderstanding: that exile might preserve ritual purity even while losing the land. The dietary laws that distinguished Israel from the nations become impossible to maintain. The very act of survival will require defilement.\
This connects to the larger sign-system: just as the siege shows God himself as the attacker, and the bound prophet shows mediation foreclosed, the dietary signs demonstrate that even basic covenant observance becomes impossible under judgment. They will "know that I am the LORD" through experiencing the systematic dismantling of every structure that maintained their identity as his people.\
Marshwiggle: Where in Torah is eating food cooked over excrement forbidden? Certainly, the people considered the nations to be unclean. The explanation for the sign of the excrement is clear enough - the people will be unclean because they will be in exile among the nations. But we should be careful to check if we are going beyond the text. Ezekiel's protest is precisely that he has not violated the list of covenant stipulations in the matter of eating defiled food. While strictly eating food prepared over a fire of human excrement is not forbidden by Torah, the laws concerning careful treatment of human excrement have that implication. And so the prophet, permitted to negotiate for himself but not for the people, is allowed this lesser defilement. Even if not covenant breaking, defilement was horrible for two reasons. First, like exile, eating food cooked over dung would be shameful. Second, defilement makes one unclean, excluded from God's presence, from the place appropriate to God's people. To what degree should this evidence alter how we see the sign of defilement, and in turn, the three signs together?\
