\cf2 The negotiation for cow dung preserves Ezekiel's personal ritual status while still demonstrating the principle: exile means living in conditions incompatible with holiness. The shame is not just social but theological - they who were meant to be a kingdom of priests will exist in states of perpetual defilement.\
This explains why they will "rot away in their iniquity" (v. 17) - not just from hunger but from the spiritual decay of being unable to approach the God whose presence defined them. The dietary signs demonstrate that judgment doesn't merely punish but dismantles the very possibility of being God's people in the way Torah envisions.\
Marshwiggle: And yet, Torah did predict that this very state of affairs. To a great degree, these signs refer to the Leviticus 26 threat against covenant breakers: And I will bring a sword upon you, that shall execute vengeance for the covenant. And if you gather within your cities, I will send pestilence among you, and you shall be delivered into the hand of the enemy. When I break your supply of bread, ten women shall bake your bread in a single oven and shall dole out your bread again by weight, and you shall eat and not be satisfied.\
Opus: The Leviticus 26 connection transforms these signs from prediction to covenant enforcement. The phrase 
\f1 \uc0\u1502 \u1463 \u1496 \u1461 \u1468 \u1492 \u1470 \u1500 \u1462 \u1495 \u1462 \u1501 
\f0  ("staff of bread") in Ezekiel 4:16 directly echoes Leviticus 26:26, and the parallel extends through the entire sequence: bread by weight, eating without satisfaction, the siege context.\
This reframes everything. Ezekiel isn't merely warning of coming disaster - he's demonstrating that God is executing the specific terms of the covenant. The 390+40 day accounting makes sense in this light: it's not arbitrary divine anger but the triggering of stipulated consequences once violations reach their prescribed measure.\
The dietary signs thus function as legal proof. When Jerusalem falls and the people eat rationed, defiled food in exile, they cannot claim God broke faith. He's executing precisely what he swore in Leviticus 26 - even the progression there from initial discipline to ultimate scattering among the nations if they persist in rebellion.\
This explains why "they will know that I am the LORD" serves as the culminating purpose. They will recognize him not just as powerful but as faithful to his word - the same God who established the covenant is the one who enforces its curses. The very precision of the judgment proves his identity as their covenant God, not despite the judgment but through it.\
Ezekiel's bound body, his siege implements, his desperate bread all make visible what Leviticus 26 had made verbal: this is what covenant breach looks like when fully realized.\
Marshwiggle: The fourth and last of these embodied signs given to Ezekiel also refers to Leviticus 26, though the metaphor of shaving for the disgrace and removal of exile comes from Isaiah 7, a different exile carried out by Assyria, perhaps distant from us, but a horrible and very concrete example. Moreover, the words for shaving hair are, perhaps, of less impact than seeing in person a man shaving hair with a sword.\
