\cf2 The physical horror of the act matches its meaning. Hair and beard represent human dignity, covenant identity, priestly consecration. To shave with a sword reduces all of this to something that must be violently removed. The few hairs preserved in Ezekiel's garment (v. 3) then cast into fire (v. 4) suggest even the remnant isn't secure.\
This final sign completes the sequence by showing not just siege and exile but the total undoing of Israel's dignified status as God's people - performed with an instrument of war that transforms grooming into near-death.\
Marshwiggle: That sword, unsheathed, is unsheathed by the LORD, as it was in Leviticus 26. Not only is the third that remains to be exiled scattered, the LORD is still their enemy. Even in the very moment where Ezekiel looks to be offering some hope to the exiles, taking a few of the scattered people into safety, some of those are taken and burned. If Ezekiel, as a priest, earlier represented the people, here he represents God. God will take the same actions Ezekiel does in this sign. The judgement to come will be as thorough as Ezekiel's weighing and division.\
