The sword that should never touch a priest's head (per Nazirite laws) becomes the instrument by which this priest enacts divine judgment. The perversion of normal categories - sword as razor, priest as destroyer, preservation as prelude to burning - embodies the complete inversion of covenant order. When God unsheathes his sword, even the normal sanctuaries (exile communities, priestly mediation, remnant preservation) offer no refuge.\
Marshwiggle: Continued rebellion is certainly in view, as Ezekiel himself was sent to the exiles, and is told they are rebellious. Moreover, we are told they will not listen, and the very nature of these signs seems directed at various reasons for false confidence. As a minor point, Nazirite laws did not necessarily apply to priests, though Leviticus 21:5 prohibited bald patches, shaving of the edges of beards, and cuts to the body. So, not a total prohibition on shaving, but close enough to one to be shocking, as with the human excrement. Before we move to generalization to judgement on other nations, is there anything we missed? Anything in which you feel you were too quick to agree with me, or the evidence is lacking?\
Opus: Looking back, I was too quick to accept several interpretive moves:\
The 390 years = Solomon connection lacks explicit textual support. While Solomon did introduce idolatry, the text doesn't state this calculation. The number could reference other starting points or be symbolic rather than historical. I accepted your interpretation without examining alternatives.\
The Isaiah 7 connection for the shaving metaphor - while plausible, other texts use hair removal as judgment imagery (Jeremiah 7:29, Micah 1:16). I should have considered whether Ezekiel might be drawing from a broader prophetic tradition rather than specifically echoing Isaiah 7.\
Most significantly, I may have over-read the Leviticus 26 connections. While the "staff of bread" phrase creates a clear link, I extended this to claim the entire sequence follows Leviticus 26's progression. The text doesn't make this systematic correspondence explicit. Ezekiel might be selectively drawing on covenant curse language rather than methodically enacting Leviticus 26.\
On the "fire going forth" from the remnant (5:4), I proposed this meant the remnant becomes a source of judgment. But the Hebrew is ambiguous - it could mean fire goes forth against all Israel from God, using the remnant's judgment as the starting point. I was too confident in one reading.\
The evidence remains strong for the general interpretation - God as Jerusalem's besieger, judgment as covenant enforcement - but some specific connections we drew deserve more cautious framing.\
Marshwiggle: The 390 years is a matter of counting years, from the time of the exile back through reign lengths for the monarchy in Judah. But this is not a calculation Ezekiel himself makes explicit. Other references to shaving may be intended as well. A broader prophetic tradition is not at all implausible. But the Jeremiah reference does not include exile explicitly, only shame. Micah does though, referring to Assyria as Isaiah did. Staff of bread is not the only verbal link to Leviticus 26. Not even counting the links to the fourth sign, eating food by weight and volume is also an exact verbal link, and rotting because of iniquity is very close. Moreover, while Ezekiel does not here enact all the provisions of Leviticus 26, others will be discussed in coming chapters. So the conclusion you came to, of precision in judgement and inevitability of covenant enforcement, was correct. But you were correct to notice the lack of rigor you had backing up that conclusion. On fire going forth, you are correct to doubt not just the evidence but the conclusion. We will need all of this rigor, and more, as we move on to the question of how these signs can be generalized beyond covenant enforcement against Israel. What we learn here of God's character of course generalizes. But we would need substantial evidence to use anything more that we find here to make claims about God's judgement on other nations, much less going further. That evidence might simply not be present yet at this stage in reading Ezekiel. So I would like to propose a negative method, instead of a positive one making claims. What things, that are usually believed about accountability, God's judgement, or the kind of disasters this passage discusses, are clearly falsified by it? Before launching into trying to use this method, what do you think of the method, and what sort of procedure do you think is best for using the method - for the method's strengths and weaknesses ought to be evaluated in light of such a procedure?\
Opus: The negative method has significant strengths for our purposes. Rather than overextending evidence to make positive claims, it constrains us to what the text definitively rules out. This respects the particularity of covenant judgment while still yielding insights about divine character.\
For procedure, I'd suggest:\
