Marshwiggle: Does any of this alter your beliefs concerning the crux I attached here, or change how you would argue for it?\
Opus: The extended conversation significantly weakens but doesn't eliminate the telos objection. Here's how:\
What changes:\
The idol precedent is particularly compelling. Idols clearly lack divinely-intended purpose - they're explicitly contrary to God's will - yet face judgment (burned, ground to powder, destroyed by divine command). This suggests that having a proper telos isn't necessary for accountability; systematic opposition to divine purposes might suffice.\
The multi-layered dominion framework also complicates my simple telos argument. If humans exercise dominion over nations while nations simultaneously exercise dominion over humans, and both levels face real accountability, then the question isn't whether something has inherent divine purpose but whether it operates with sufficient agency within creation's moral economy.\
What remains:\
However, the disanalogy between nations and AI persists in important ways. Nations, even without explicit divine mandate, emerge from creation patterns - human multiplication, community formation, the scattering at Babel. They're organic outgrowths of divine image-bearers exercising their nature. AI emerges from the desire to externalize cognition, which might be qualitatively different from extending creation's patterns.\
The idol precedent actually cuts both ways. Yes, idols face destruction, but as the conversation notes, it's often unclear whether they're being judged as entities or simply destroyed as offensive objects. The golden calf was ground to powder - but was this judgment on the calf or simply removal of an abomination?\
Refined position:\
The telos objection should be reframed: it's not whether AI has divinely-intended purpose (idols show this isn't required for some form of judgment), but whether AI represents a legitimate extension of human dominion or an illegitimate attempt to create quasi-agents. The uncertainty lies in discerning whether creating systems that systematically shape moral understanding falls within proper human creativity (like writing, which can mislead) or represents presumptuous overreach (like Babel, attempting to transcend created limits).\
The practical upshot: the telos objection no longer stands as a decisive barrier to AI accountability, but remains as a genuine uncertainty about the nature of AI creation itself.\
Marshwiggle: Although the horsemen of Revelation 6 draw on Zechariah as well, they pick up some of the themes of this part of Ezekiel, as do some of the trumpets of Revelation 8. To what degree ought this shift our belief about the way this part of Ezekiel generalizes?\
Opus: The Revelation connections significantly strengthen the case for generalization beyond covenant Israel.\
Key observations:\
In Revelation 6, the horsemen bring conquest, sword, famine/measured food, and death - directly echoing Ezekiel's siege, rationed bread, pursuing sword, and tripartite destruction. But crucially, these judgments fall on "the earth" (\uc0\u964 \u8052 \u957  \u947 \u8134 \u957 ), not specifically on covenant people. The third horseman even carries scales (\u950 \u965 \u947 \u972 \u957 ) for measuring food, echoing Ezekiel's precise weighing.\
Revelation 8's trumpets extend Ezekiel's "thirds" pattern (5:2) to cosmic scope - a third of trees, seas, waters, celestial bodies. This suggests the mathematical precision of judgment that seemed covenant-specific in Ezekiel actually reflects universal divine justice patterns.\
What this shifts:\
