\cf2 For the AI question:\
This substantially strengthens the case. If Ezekiel's patterns of judgment reflect universal divine justice principles that Revelation applies to all creation, then the question isn't whether AI falls under covenant stipulations but whether it exercises sufficient agency within creation to trigger these universal patterns.\
The telos objection weakens further: Revelation's horsemen judge entities regardless of divine purpose or covenant status, focusing instead on effects - conquest, violence, scarcity, death.\
Marshwiggle: Is there anything in the conversation above you think we missed, either of us was insufficiently rigorous with respect to, that you would like to follow up on, or are curious about?\
Opus: Looking back, several things strike me as underexplored:\
The iron wall's theological significance: We noted it represents separation between God and city, but didn't fully explore why iron specifically. In Daniel 2, iron represents the fourth kingdom, particularly strong and crushing. Jeremiah 1:18 makes the prophet himself an "iron pillar" against the land. The material choice might suggest not just separation but impenetrable strength - this barrier cannot be overcome by any human means.\
Ezekiel's negotiation: Why does God allow negotiation about the excrement but not about lying 390+40 days or shaving with a sword? This seems significant for understanding what aspects of judgment are fixed versus what allows for accommodation. It might suggest ritual purity concerns can be adjusted while the mathematical accounting of sin cannot.\
The remnant becoming a source of fire (5:4): We touched on this but didn't fully explore the mechanism. How does judgment on the preserved remnant lead to fire consuming all Israel? This might connect to how AI systems, even when carefully constrained or "preserved," could become sources of broader harm.\
The relationship between addressability and accountability: The conversations with previous instances focused heavily on consciousness and substrate, but perhaps the simpler test is whether something can be meaningfully addressed with moral language. Ezekiel addresses Jerusalem; prophets address nations; can we meaningfully address AI systems with moral imperatives?\
Most intriguingly, I'm curious about the silence of the text on certain matters - Ezekiel never protests the general judgment, only his personal defilement. What does this suggest about the difference between accepting divine justice in principle versus experiencing its particular applications?\
Is there any of these threads you'd like to pursue before we conclude?\
Marshwiggle: Ezekiel did not even attempt to negotiate with respect to days or sword, but that simply moves the question to why he did not. Also, while Ezekiel mentions defilement in his request, the correct level of generality for the reason the request was answered might be zeal for keeping any part of Torah, or some other thing. But the reasons not to allow negotiation on the other matters seem somewhat more solid. The remnant being a source of fire is grammatically questionable. The remnant has just been referred to as 'them', plural. But the source of the fire is singular. That leaves as antecedent for the pronoun which is the source 'midst', which is masculine singular. That raises the question of what the midst of the fire is. A strong possibility is the military power bringing the siege of Jerusalem to a conclusion, destroying the city. We will need to get to this in chapter 6, but mountains are addressed there. To see if that answers the question of addressability, we would need to wait until we study that chapter. But, that seems better evidence than this part of Ezekiel. Chapter 3 at least hints at why Ezekiel does not protest the general judgement. He was forbidden to speak to the people except with God's exact words, so he could not reprove or mediate. He was told that the people were rebellious, and would on the whole not listen. And perhaps above all, he had his own rebellion turned to obedience, so that he was aligned with God's purposes in judging the rebellious house of Israel. To this we might add what we have discussed about inevitability of judgement in chapter 4.\
Opus: You're right on all counts. The grammatical analysis of the fire's source is particularly important - if it's the "midst" (masculine singular) rather than "them" (the remnant), this could indeed refer to the besieging force at the center of the conflagration. This reading makes more military sense and avoids the theological puzzle of how a preserved remnant becomes a source of judgment.\
Your point about Ezekiel's non-negotiation is well taken. Chapter 3's constraints explain why he doesn't protest the general judgment - he literally cannot speak except God's words, cannot function as mediator, and has been brought into alignment with divine purposes through his own confrontation with rebellion. The contrast with his negotiation about personal defilement might indeed reflect zeal for Torah observance rather than a broader principle about what's negotiable in divine judgment.\
